https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/undergrad/pastpapers/files/2011/PaperIA_1.pdf

= 1C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= Solution
{parent=1C}

For a nonzero <complex number>, write $z=re^{i\theta}$ with $r=|z|>0$ and principal <complex argument> $-\pi<\theta\le\pi$. The <principal complex logarithm> and corresponding <complex exponentiation> are
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Log}z=\log r+i\theta,\qquad z^a=\exp(a\operatorname{Log}z).}
$$
The value at zero is not defined by this formula. The choice of <complex argument> matters: these are single-valued principal powers, rather than all values of a multivalued <complex logarithm>.

For the requested sketch, $(1+i)\operatorname{Log}z=(\log r-\theta)+i(\log r+\theta)$. Its <complex exponential> has <complex modulus> $e^{\log r-\theta}$, so
$$
\boxed{|z^{1+i}|=1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad r=e^\theta,\qquad-\pi<\theta\le\pi.}
$$
This is one turn of a <logarithmic spiral>, with increasing radius as the polar angle increases. It passes through $1$ at $\theta=0$, approaches the excluded endpoint $-e^{-\pi}$ from below the negative real axis, and ends at the included endpoint $-e^\pi$ from above that axis. The endpoint distinction follows from the chosen principal <complex argument>.
\Image[paper-1-principal-spiral.png]
{title=One turn of the principal-power logarithmic spiral, with open inner and closed outer endpoints}

= i
{parent=1c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The principal <complex exponentiation> gives $z^i=e^{-\theta}e^{i\log r}$. Equality to one requires its <complex modulus> to be one, hence $\theta=0$, and then its <complex argument> requires $\log r\in2\pi\mathbb Z$. Thus **all solutions are positive real**, namely
$$
\boxed{z=e^{2\pi k},\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Substitution verifies every value, and the modulus condition excludes every nonreal value.

= ii
{parent=1c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Away from the negative real axis, the <complex conjugate> has principal <complex argument> $-\theta$. Consequently
$$
z^i+\overline z^{\,i}=(e^{-\theta}+e^\theta)e^{i\log r}=2\cosh\theta\,e^{i\log r}.
$$
Taking the <complex modulus> gives $\cosh\theta=1$, hence $\theta=0$. The remaining equation is $e^{i\log r}=i$, so
$$
\boxed{z=e^{\pi/2+2\pi k},\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
On the negative real axis, both $z$ and $\overline z$ have principal <complex argument> $\pi$, so the left-hand side has modulus $2e^{-\pi}$, not two. There are no additional solutions there. The printed second term is the principal power of $\overline z$, not the <complex conjugate> of $z^i$.

= 2A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= Solution
{parent=2A}

Use <change of basis> matrices whose columns are the respective <basis vectors> in standard coordinates:
$$
S_B=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&-1\end{pmatrix},\quad
S_C=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\1&1&1\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
S_{B'}=\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\2&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
S_{C'}=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&1\\0&1&2\\-1&0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The coordinate relation is $[\Phi(x)]_C=A[x]_B$. Therefore the standard-coordinate <matrix of a linear map> is $T=S_CAS_B^{-1}$, and the new coordinate matrix is $A'=S_{C'}^{-1}TS_{B'}$. Multiplying gives
$$
T=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\2&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
TS_{B'}=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&4\\-2&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\boxed{A'=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&4\\0&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
One can also read off the columns: the two new domain <basis vectors> map to twice the first and four times the second new codomain <basis vector>. All four <change of basis> matrices are invertible. In particular the printed codomain vectors are three-dimensional; retaining their third coordinates is essential.

= 3F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= a
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem> states that **every bounded <sequence> in $\mathbb R$ has a convergent <subsequence>**. More generally, a bounded <sequence> in $\mathbb R^d$ has a <subsequence> converging to a point of $\mathbb R^d$.

= b
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take $a_n=n$. Every <subsequence> has the form $a_{n_k}=n_k$, with $n_k\ge k$, so it tends to positive infinity and cannot converge to a real number. Thus **this <sequence> has no convergent <subsequence>**.

= c
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Define $a_{2k}=0$ and $a_{2k-1}=k$. The odd-indexed terms are unbounded, while the even-indexed <subsequence> is identically zero and hence converges to zero. This gives **an unbounded <sequence> with a convergent <subsequence>**.

= d
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <floor function> has even value on indices congruent to zero or one modulo four, and odd value on indices congruent to two or three. Accordingly the displayed <sequence> has terms $2+1/n$ in the first two residue classes and $-1/n$ in the other two. In particular,
$$
\boxed{a_{4k}=2+\frac1{4k}\longrightarrow2,\qquad
a_{4k+2}=-\frac1{4k+2}\longrightarrow0.}
$$
Every term is within $1/n$ of the set $\{0,2\}$. If a <subsequence> converged to $c\notin\{0,2\}$, its eventual distance from that set would be bounded below by a positive constant, contradicting this bound. **The complete set of subsequential limits is $\{0,2\}$.**

= 4D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= i
{parent=4d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <power series> coefficient $c_n=n^2$, the <Cauchy-Hadamard theorem> gives
$$
R^{-1}=\limsup_{n\to\infty}|c_n|^{1/n}
=\lim_{n\to\infty}\exp\!\left(\frac{2\log n}{n}\right)=1.
$$
Thus the **<radius of convergence> is $\boxed{R=1}$**. The <power series> converges absolutely for $|z|<1$ and diverges for $|z|>1$. On $|z|=1$ its terms have modulus $n^2$, so they do not tend to zero and the series diverges there as well.

= ii
{parent=4d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The original PDF has coefficient $c_n=n^{\,n^{1/3}}$. Its $n$th root is
$$
|c_n|^{1/n}=n^{n^{-2/3}}
=\exp\!\left(\frac{\log n}{n^{2/3}}\right)\longrightarrow1.
$$
The <Cauchy-Hadamard theorem> therefore gives **$\boxed{R=1}$**. Again the <power series> converges absolutely inside the unit circle, while on that circle its terms do not tend to zero. The converted TeX loses the outer exponent and writes $n^{1/3}$; the calculation here uses the printed coefficient.

= 5C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= Solution
{parent=5C}

In the parametric description, $a$ is a point of the <straight line> and the nonzero <vector> $b$ gives its direction. The real parameter runs through every point of that line. For the <cross product> description, take another <cross product> with $c$ and use the <vector triple product identity>:
$$
c\times(x\times c)=|c|^2x-(c\cdot x)c=c\times d.
$$
Hence every solution has the representation
$$
\boxed{x=a+\lambda(x)b,\qquad
a=\frac{c\times d}{|c|^2},\qquad b=c,\qquad
\lambda(x)=\frac{c\cdot x}{|c|^2}.}
$$
Here $a\cdot b=0$ and $|b|=|c|$, as required. Conversely,
$$
(a+\lambda c)\times c
=\frac{(c\times d)\times c}{|c|^2}
=d-\frac{(c\cdot d)c}{|c|^2}=d.
$$
Thus the perpendicularity assumption is sufficient, and the solutions form a <straight line> parallel to $c$. When $d\ne0$, the <vector> $d$ is perpendicular both to $c$ and to every position <vector> on the line; it is the normal to the plane through the origin containing that line. The point $a=c\times d/|c|^2$ is the perpendicular foot from the origin. Its distance from the origin is $|d|/|c|$, since $c\cdot d=0$.

For a general parametric <straight line>, minimize the squared <Euclidean distance>
$$
|a+\lambda b-y|^2=|a-y|^2+2\lambda b\cdot(a-y)+\lambda^2|b|^2.
$$
This strictly convex <quadratic function> has its unique minimum at $\lambda=b\cdot(y-a)/|b|^2$, giving the <orthogonal projection>
$$
\boxed{x_{\rm nearest}=a+\frac{b\cdot(y-a)}{|b|^2}b.}
$$
For the <cross product> description, $a\cdot c=0$, so this becomes
$$
\boxed{x_{\rm nearest}=\frac{c\times d}{|c|^2}+\frac{c\cdot y}{|c|^2}c.}
$$

For the two planes, put $s=m\cdot n$ and $c=m\times n$. Nonparallel unit normals give $|s|<1$ and $|c|^2=1-s^2$. Seek the perpendicular foot in their span, $a=um+vn$. Its two <dot products> impose $u+sv=\mu$ and $su+v=\nu$. Solving gives both requested descriptions of the intersection:
$$
\boxed{x=\frac{(\mu-s\nu)m+(\nu-s\mu)n}{1-s^2}+\lambda(m\times n),\qquad\lambda\in\mathbb R,}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{x\times(m\times n)=\nu m-\mu n.}
$$
Indeed the <vector triple product identity> gives $x\times(m\times n)=m(x\cdot n)-n(x\cdot m)$. Conversely, since $m,n$ are linearly independent, equality to $\nu m-\mu n$ forces both plane equations. Thus neither description introduces extra points.

= 6A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= a
{parent=6a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <vector triple product identity> gives $n\times(x\times n)=x-(n\cdot x)n$. Decompose $x=x_\perp+x_\parallel$, where $x_\parallel=(n\cdot x)n$ and $x_\perp\cdot n=0$. Then
$$
\Phi(x)=x_\perp+\alpha x_\parallel.
$$
The <linear map> acts as the identity on $n^\perp$ and as multiplication by $\alpha$ along $n$. Consequently **it is invertible exactly when $\alpha\ne0$**, with
$$
\boxed{\Phi^{-1}(y)=y+\left(\alpha^{-1}-1\right)(n\cdot y)n.}
$$
Applying $\Phi$ to this expression recovers the perpendicular and parallel components of $y$ separately.

= b
{parent=6a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At $\alpha=0$, $\Phi$ is the <orthogonal projection> onto the plane perpendicular to $n$. Its <image of a linear map> and <kernel of a linear map> are
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{im}\Phi=n^\perp,\qquad \ker\Phi=\operatorname{span}\{n\}.}
$$
Every perpendicular <vector> is unchanged and every parallel <vector> is annihilated. The image plane is perpendicular to the kernel axis by the definition of $n^\perp$; their dimensions two and one also agree with the <rank-nullity theorem>.

= c
{parent=6a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Expanding the formula from part (a) in components gives $y_i=x_i+(\alpha-1)n_i n_jx_j$, with repeated indices summed. Hence the <matrix of a linear map> and its inverse, when $\alpha\ne0$, have entries
$$
\boxed{A_{ij}=\delta_{ij}+(\alpha-1)n_i n_j,\qquad
B_{ij}=\delta_{ij}+(\alpha^{-1}-1)n_i n_j.}
$$
Here $\delta_{ij}$ is the <Kronecker delta>. To check the <matrix inverse>, write $P=nn^T$; $P^2=P$ because $|n|=1$. Thus $A=(I-P)+\alpha P$ and $B=(I-P)+\alpha^{-1}P$, whose product is the <identity matrix>.

= d
{parent=6a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For the specified unit <vector>,
$$
A-C=\frac13\begin{pmatrix}
\alpha&\alpha-3&\alpha\\
\alpha&\alpha&\alpha-3\\
\alpha-3&\alpha&\alpha
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For a cyclic <matrix> with first row $(a,b,c)$, its <determinant> is $a^3+b^3+c^3-3abc$. Here it gives
$$
\det(A-C)=\frac{2\alpha^3+(\alpha-3)^3-3\alpha^2(\alpha-3)}{27}=\alpha-1.
$$
Therefore $Ax=Cx$ has only the zero solution if $\alpha\ne1$. At $\alpha=1$, $A=I$ and $A-C$ has kernel consisting of the equal-coordinate <vectors>. The complete answer is
$$
\boxed{\alpha\ne1:\ x=0;\qquad \alpha=1:\ x=t(1,1,1),\quad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Geometrically, $C^TC=I$, $\det C=1$ and $Cn=n$, so $C$ is a <rotation matrix> about the $n$ axis. Its <matrix trace> is two, giving $\cos\theta=(\operatorname{tr}C-1)/2=1/2$; its skew part selects angle $-\pi/3$ about the oriented axis $n$. It has no fixed nonzero perpendicular <vector>. Meanwhile $A$ preserves perpendicular components and scales the axial component by $\alpha$. Comparing these components first forces $x_\perp=0$, and then $(\alpha-1)x_\parallel=0$, exactly matching the <determinant> calculation, including the noninvertible case $\alpha=0$.

= 7B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= a
{parent=7b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Expanding the <characteristic polynomial> gives
$$
\det(M-tI)=(2-t)\bigl[(1-t)(3-t)+2\bigr]-2(2-t)
=(2-t)(1-t)(3-t).
$$
Thus the <eigenvalues> are $1,2,3$. Solving the corresponding <homogeneous linear systems> gives
$$
\boxed{E_1=\operatorname{span}\{(-1,0,1)^T\},\quad
E_2=\operatorname{span}\{(1,1,0)^T\},\quad
E_3=\operatorname{span}\{(1,1,1)^T\}.}
$$
Every nonzero <vector> in the displayed <eigenspace> is an <eigenvector>. Direct multiplication verifies the three eigenvalue equations. The distinct <eigenvalues> imply these <eigenvectors> are linearly independent and form a <basis> of $\mathbb R^3$.

= b
{parent=7b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The equation is solvable exactly when $b\in\operatorname{im}A$, the <image of a linear map>, equivalently when the <rank of a matrix> satisfies $\operatorname{rank}A=\operatorname{rank}[A\mid b]$. If it is solvable and $x_0$ is one solution, every solution is precisely
$$
x=x_0+v,\qquad v\in\ker A.
$$
Indeed differences of solutions lie in the <kernel of a linear map>, and adding a kernel <vector> preserves the equation. Hence **there are no solutions** when $b\notin\operatorname{im}A$; **one solution** when $A$ is invertible; and **infinitely many solutions** when $b\in\operatorname{im}A$ and $A$ is singular. In the last case the <rank-nullity theorem> gives a nonzero kernel <vector> $v$, and $x_0+tv$ gives distinct solutions for every real $t$. This proves that a finite number greater than one is impossible.

= c
{parent=7b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the <eigenvector> <basis> $v_1=(-1,0,1)^T$, $v_2=(1,1,0)^T$, $v_3=(1,1,1)^T$. The forcing has decomposition $b=-v_1+3v_3$. If $x=c_1v_1+c_2v_2+c_3v_3$, the <linear system> reduces to
$$
(1-\lambda)c_1=-1,\qquad(2-\lambda)c_2=0,\qquad(3-\lambda)c_3=3.
$$
For $\lambda=0$, all coefficients are determined, giving
$$
\boxed{x=(2,1,0)^T.}
$$
For $\lambda=1$, the first equation would say $0=-1$, so **there are no solutions**. For $\lambda=2$, $c_1=1$, $c_3=3$, and $c_2$ is arbitrary. Thus **all solutions** are
$$
\boxed{x=(2,3,4)^T+t(1,1,0)^T,\qquad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$
These outcomes illustrate respectively an invertible <matrix>, incompatible forcing outside its <image of a linear map>, and a compatible forcing with a nontrivial <kernel of a linear map>.

= 8B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Vectors and Matrices}

= a
{parent=8b}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $Mv=\lambda v$ with $v\in\mathbb C^n$ nonzero. For a real <symmetric matrix>, $M^*=M$, where the star denotes the <conjugate transpose>. Consequently $v^*Mv$ equals its <complex conjugate> and is real. The <eigenvalue equation> gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\frac{v^*Mv}{v^*v}\in\mathbb R,}
$$
since $v^*v>0$. Allowing a complex <eigenvector> in this argument is essential: reality of an <eigenvalue> must be proved, rather than assumed in advance.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write a complex <eigenvector> as $v=u+iw$, where $u,w\in\mathbb R^n$. Since $M$ and its <eigenvalue> $\lambda$ are real,
$$
Mu=\lambda u,\qquad Mw=\lambda w.
$$
At least one of $u,w$ is nonzero, and it is a real <eigenvector> for the same <eigenvalue>. More generally every complex <eigenvector> is a complex linear combination of real ones: its real and imaginary parts lie in the real <eigenspace>. Thus **each <eigenspace> has a <basis> chosen over the reals**. The conclusion does not assert that every arbitrary complex <eigenvector> is itself real.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Choose real <eigenvectors> $u,v$ with distinct <eigenvalues> $\lambda,\mu$. Symmetry gives
$$
\lambda u^Tv=(Mu)^Tv=u^TMv=\mu u^Tv.
$$
Therefore $(\lambda-\mu)u^Tv=0$, and **$\boxed{u\cdot v=0}$**. For complex choices, replace transpose by <conjugate transpose>; the reality proved in part (i) gives the same conclusion for the <Hermitian inner product>. Thus different <eigenspaces> are <orthogonal>.

= b
{parent=8b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a real <antisymmetric matrix>, $A^T=-A$, so $(A^2)^T=A^2$. The previous result makes every <eigenvalue> $\eta$ of $A^2$ real, with a real nonzero <eigenvector> $v$. Moreover,
$$
\eta|v|^2=v^TA^2v=-(Av)^TAv=-|Av|^2\le0.
$$
Thus **every <eigenvalue> of $A^2$ is real and nonpositive**.

Take real unit <eigenvectors> $u,w$ of $A^2$ for the respective distinct nonzero <eigenvalues> $-\lambda^2,-\mu^2$, where the real parameters $\lambda,\mu$ are nonzero. Define
$$
u'=\frac{Au}{\lambda},\qquad w'=\frac{Aw}{\mu}.
$$
The displayed quadratic identity gives $|u'|=|w'|=1$. Also $u\cdot Au=0$ and $w\cdot Aw=0$, because a real <antisymmetric matrix> has zero <quadratic form>. Thus $u\perp u'$ and $w\perp w'$. Since $A$ commutes with $A^2$, $u'$ lies in the same <eigenspace> of $A^2$ as $u$, and $w'$ in the same one as $w$. Distinct <eigenspaces> of the real <symmetric matrix> $A^2$ are <orthogonal>, so all four <vectors> are <orthonormal>. Finally,
$$
\boxed{Au=\lambda u',\quad Au'=-\lambda u,\qquad
Aw=\mu w',\quad Aw'=-\mu w.}
$$
Thus on each of the two perpendicular planes, $A$ acts as a scaled quarter-turn. This constructs the required <vectors>, rather than merely counting dimensions.

= 9F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= a
{parent=9f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a positive-term <series>, the <ratio test> says that if $a_{n+1}/a_n\to L<1$, the series converges; if the ratio tends to $L>1$, including positive infinity, it diverges. More generally $\limsup a_{n+1}/a_n<1$ suffices for convergence and $\liminf a_{n+1}/a_n>1$ for divergence. **A limiting ratio of one gives no conclusion.**

The <P-series> $\sum n^{-2}$ converges and the <harmonic series> $\sum n^{-1}$ diverges. Both have strictly decreasing positive terms, and their successive ratios, $(n/(n+1))^2$ and $n/(n+1)$, tend to one. These supply the two requested possibilities.

= b
{parent=9f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <function> $1/x$ is decreasing and positive. Therefore on $[k,k+1]$ it is at most $1/k$, and the <Riemann integral> gives
$$
\int_k^{k+1}\frac{dx}{x}\le\frac1k.
$$
Summing from $k=1$ to $n-1$ yields
$$
\boxed{\log n=\int_1^n\frac{dx}{x}\le\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\frac1k.}
$$
For $n=1$ both sides are zero; for $n>1$ the inequality is actually strict. The same <integral test for convergence> gives $\sum_{k=N}^{n-1}1/k\ge\log(n/N)$.

= c
{parent=9f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

First, for $0<x<1$, the <derivative> of $h(x)=\log(1-x)+x$ is $-x/(1-x)<0$, while $h(0)=0$. Hence $\log(1-x)<-x$.

Choose $N>c$ large enough that the given ratio bound holds for every $n\ge N$. Positivity permits taking <logarithms>, and the preceding inequality gives
$$
\log a_{k+1}-\log a_k\le\log(1-c/k)<-c/k.
$$
Summing, and using the <integral test for convergence> from part (b), gives for $n>N$
$$
\log a_n\le\log a_N-c\sum_{k=N}^{n-1}\frac1k
\le\log a_N-c\log(n/N).
$$
Thus $a_n\le a_NN^c n^{-c}$. Since $c>1$, the <P-series> $\sum n^{-c}$ converges, so the <comparison test for series> proves
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n<\infty.}
$$
The finitely many initial terms do not affect convergence. This <power-law comparison from a refined ratio bound> supplies information when the ordinary <ratio test> can be inconclusive.

= 10E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= Solution
{parent=10E}

The <intermediate value theorem> states that if $f:[a,b]\to\mathbb R$ is <continuous>, then every real value between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ is attained at some $c\in[a,b]$. Here is a proof using <completeness of the real numbers>. Endpoint values are immediate, so suppose $f(a)<y<f(b)$; reversing the sign of $f$ handles the other ordering. Let
$$
S=\{x\in[a,b]:f(x)<y\},\qquad c=\sup S.
$$
The set is nonempty and bounded above. <Continuity> at $a$ puts points to the right of $a$ in $S$, while <continuity> at $b$ excludes an entire interval to the left of $b$, so $a<c<b$. If $f(c)<y$, <continuity> puts some point larger than $c$ in $S$, contradicting its <supremum>. If $f(c)>y$, <continuity> excludes $S$ in a neighborhood of $c$, contradicting the existence of points of $S$ arbitrarily close to its <supremum> from below. Therefore $f(c)=y$, proving the theorem.

For the fixed-point assertion, put $g(x)=f(x)-x$. This is <continuous>, with $g(0)=f(0)\ge0$ and $g(1)=f(1)-1\le0$. If either endpoint value is zero it already gives a <fixed point>; otherwise the <intermediate value theorem> gives an interior zero. Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{Every continuous self-map of }[0,1]\text{ has a fixed point.}}
$$
The proof needs both <continuity> and the closed-interval endpoint information; the following counterexamples separate those hypotheses.

= i
{parent=10e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**No.** The <continuous function> $f(x)=(x+1)/2$ maps $(0,1)$ into $(0,1)$, but $f(x)-x=(1-x)/2>0$ throughout the domain. Its only possible <fixed point> is the excluded endpoint one.

= ii
{parent=10e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No.** The <continuous function> $f(x)=x+1$ is a self-map of $\mathbb R$, but the <fixed point> equation would require $x+1=x$, which is impossible.

= iii
{parent=10e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**No.** Define $f(x)=1$ for $0\le x<1$ and $f(1)=0$. This self-map of $[0,1]$ is discontinuous at one. Every point smaller than one is sent to a different point, and one is sent to zero, so it has no <fixed point>.

= iv
{parent=10e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**Yes: a tangential zero and a crossing zero can give exactly two <fixed points>.** One explicit <continuous> piecewise linear example is
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\begin{cases}
1-2x,&0\le x\le1/3,\\
\frac52x-\frac12,&1/3\le x\le1/2,\\
1-\frac12x,&1/2\le x\le2/3,\\
2-2x,&2/3\le x\le1.
\end{cases}}
$$
Adjacent formulas agree at each junction. Their endpoint values lie in $[0,1]$, so linear interpolation proves that the whole image lies in $[0,1]$; also $f(0)=1$ and $f(1)=0$. On the four pieces, $f(x)-x$ is respectively $1-3x$, $3x/2-1/2$, $1-3x/2$, and $2-3x$. Their only zeros on their respective intervals are $1/3$ and $2/3$. Thus **the complete <fixed point> set is $\boxed{\{1/3,2/3\}}$**. Continuity forces at least one zero, but does not force every zero to change sign.

= 11E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis I}

= Solution
{parent=11E}

For the first additional construction, modify the rational/irrational example to
$$
\boxed{g(x)=\begin{cases}x^2,&x\in\mathbb Q,\\-x^2,&x\notin\mathbb Q.\end{cases}}
$$
At zero, $|g(h)/h|=|h|\to0$, so $g'(0)=0$. At any nonzero point, <density of the rational numbers> and <density of the irrational numbers> give incompatible limiting values $x^2$ and $-x^2$. Thus $g$ is discontinuous, and hence not <differentiable>, there. **Its <differentiability> set is exactly $\{0\}$.**

For the second construction, use a <summable absolute-value cusp series>:
$$
\boxed{G(x)=\sum_{n=2}^\infty2^{-n}\left|x-\frac1n\right|.}
$$
On $[-R,R]$, each summand is bounded by $2^{-n}(R+1/2)$. The <Weierstrass M-test> therefore gives <uniform convergence> on every bounded interval, and the <uniform limit theorem> makes $G$ <continuous> everywhere.

To justify <differentiability>, each summand's <difference quotient> has absolute value at most $2^{-n}$, by the <reverse triangle inequality>. The sum of these bounds is finite, uniformly in the increment. Thus the tail of the <difference quotient> is uniformly small, and limits may be passed through the sum by first retaining finitely many terms and then making the tail small. If $x\ne1/n$ for every $n\ge2$, each individual summand is <differentiable>, giving
$$
G'(x)=\sum_{n=2}^\infty2^{-n}\operatorname{sgn}(x-1/n).
$$
In particular **$G'(0)=-\sum_{n=2}^\infty2^{-n}=-1/2$**. The accumulation of cusp locations at zero does not destroy the <derivative>, because their weights are summable.

At $x=1/m$, the same tail argument applies to both one-sided <derivatives>. Every term except the $m$th has the same derivative from both sides; the $m$th has derivatives $-2^{-m}$ and $2^{-m}$. Consequently
$$
G'_+(1/m)-G'_-(1/m)=2^{1-m}>0.
$$
Therefore **$G$ fails to be <differentiable> exactly at $1/2,1/3,1/4,\ldots$, and is <differentiable> everywhere else**.

= i
{parent=11e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

At zero, $|f(x)-f(0)|=|x|\to0$, so the <function> is <continuous>. At a nonzero $x_0$, approach along rational and irrational <sequences>; <density of the rational numbers> and <density of the irrational numbers> give limiting values $x_0$ and $-x_0$, which are different. Thus the **<continuity> set is $\boxed{\{0\}}$**.

At zero, the <difference quotient> is one for rational nonzero increments and minus one for irrational increments, so the <derivative> does not exist. Elsewhere <differentiability implies continuity>, and continuity already fails. Thus the **<differentiability> set is $\boxed{\varnothing}$**.

